  "notes": "Moves the cell from 5 <= chi(|T5>^2) <= 8 to 6 <= chi <= 8 (the upper bound is the annealed rank-8 witness bounds/T5-m2-upper-8.json), and by projection puts the first lower bounds on the T5 m = 3 and m = 4 cells (T5-m3-lower-6.json, T5-m4-lower-6.json). The argument (docs/notes/t5q_m2_rank5_exclusion.md) depends on: (1) ranks 1 to 4 excluded, by bounds/T5-m2-lower-5.json (numerical, with margins) and independently by the exact k = 1..4 censuses of this pipeline, so that a rank-5 decomposition has five distinct independent states and five nonzero coefficients and is a 5-set whose span contains the target; no slicing, base point, invisible term, coefficient family or degenerate list enters. (2) The pivot and partner reductions of the 5-cover enumerators (the H^6 census argument, proved in the note for a subgroup of the pivot's stabilizer): the unitary group only, antiunitary symmetries unused. (3) Exactness: every amplitude of the 3,900 states and of the target is 0 or a fifth root of unity times a column scalar (asserted for every column), both primes are 1 mod 5, the kernel's superset is keyed over F_65521 and decided modulo 2013265921, and every listed set is re-decided exactly and numerically in the batch and again by the certificate; the one unverified step is the usual census assumption that no minor used in the modular reductions vanishes modulo 65521 while nonzero over Q(zeta_5). (4) Controls (results/control_*.json): 24 planted five-term targets (random, two equal coefficients, one Pauli orbit, a repeated state, a dependent 5-set) recovered through the kernel and 8 through its Python reference, the target pattern w_5^(x^3 + y^3), the k = 1..4 censuses empty, the ten rank-3 decompositions of |T5> at m = 1 (two orbits of five under the order-5 unitary group) hit by the k = 3 census, the dictionary and unit lists re-enumerated by hash, and native against reference on sampled units."
